  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nShowers remain likely tonight into Saturday morning as low level \nmoisture deepens, roughly the lowest 5 km, and marginally dry, \ncooler air above this layer allows for weak instability, generally \n200-500 J/kg CAPE. This should generate mainly showers, but a few \nlightning strikes will be possible. Rainfall amounts will mostly be \nlight, but some pockets of a tenth of two will be possible.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday should be mostly dry, but moist neutral \nsoundings and lingering moisture/cloud cover may allow for some \npatchy drizzle and showers. Overall low impact, but likely not an \nentirely dry period.\n\nA fairly well agreed upon trough of low pressure will move onto the \nPlains Sunday night, deepening on Monday as a strong southern stream \njet strengthens. This should provide that area with widespread \nrainfall and if everything comes together timingwise, the threat for \nan isolated tornado over southeast parts of the area.\n\nAll models continue to indicate a widespread 1-2\" of rainfall from \nSunday night into Tuesday morning. Very little disagreement for the \nmost part on timing and location of the rainfall. Given a fairly wet \npattern the past 6 weeks or so river flooding could start to become \na problem. But for now, the latest hydrologic modeling suggests at \nleast 2.5-4\" of rain would be needed to push rivers to action stage \nand minor flooding, so for now chances of river flooding are pretty \nlow (<20 percent). Also suggestions that 1.5\" of rain in an hour \ncould produce some localized flash flooding, but for now the \nrainfall rates should not be quite that high. All in all, it looks \nlike a band of warm advection rainfall will lift northward Sunday \nnight into Monday. Later Monday afternoon into Monday night a dry \nslot could move in and if convection does not develop, a dry period \nor period of drizzle will be possible. Then later Monday night into \nTuesday wrap around rainfall will move through. \n\nWhile this is a very low threat probability, we will need to keep an \neye on the development of surface low pressure Monday afternoon as \nthe upper level wave moves through. Starting to see indications this \nsurface low pressure could end up near southeast SD/northwest IA \nwhich could bring about a threat for some low topped \nsupercells/isolated tornadoes. The 6z ensemble data has the EC \nplacing this SFC low in southeast SD late Monday afternoon while the \nGEFS and Canadian Ensemble are in eastern NE. Obviously a big \ndifference so trends will need to be monitored. The 12z GFS is now \nplacing the surface low in southeast SD late Monday afternoon as \nwell.\n\nTuesday will likely see some wrap around rainfall continue, but \nshould diminish throughout the day. Wednesday through Friday should \nbe mostly dry and cooler with highs from the upper 40s to mid 50s.",
